Haakon Studebaker wrote:
> I figure it's pretty simple to implement.
> 
> Hawaiian shirts are colorful. I propose that rather than having all 
> Hawaiian shirts being displayed as royal purple (gnome king, dwarf king, 
> queen bee) the color is randomized among various bright colors.

All objects (or rather object classes) have their color hard coded;
for the object class of all Hawaiian shirt instances it is magenta.
I doubt it's "pretty simple" to implement varying colors with every
object instance. As far as I understand the code correctly the color
information will be retrieved from the object class (as defined in the
"Hawaiian shirt" record), not to the individual instance of an object
(see file mapglyph.c and objects.c). Since the function mapglyph()
is generic with respect to specific objects it would likely require
a rather ugly code change. (And the reason for that change to make
shirts more colorful?)

